
Practice: Data Import/Export and Backup in PostgreSQL

Hands-on practice for the topic "Data Import/Export and Backup".

Files
File	What it does
practice_copy.sql	Creates an employees table and runs CSV export and import with \copy
practice_backup.sh	Creates backups with pg_dump / pg_dumpall, checks them, and restores into a test database
Requirements
PostgreSQL (ON_ERROR ignore needs version 17+; that line is commented out in the script)
Client tools psql, pg_dump, pg_restore, createdb, dropdb available in PATH
An empty practice database named practice_db
How to run
Create the database:
bash
   createdb -U postgres practice_db
Run the SQL script (CSV files are created in the current folder):
bash
   psql -U postgres -d practice_db -f practice_copy.sql
Create the backups (first replace USER_NAME at the top of the script):
bash
   chmod +x practice_backup.sh
   ./practice_backup.sh

Backups are saved to ~/pg_backups.

What to check
In practice_copy.sql, the row counts of employees and employees_copy match (5 and 5).
The row with a NULL salary is still NULL after import, not the text N/A.
In practice_backup.sh, the row counts in practice_db and test_restore are the same.
Notes
\copy runs on the client side and needs no superuser rights. Plain COPY reads and writes files on the server.
The backup script deletes .dump files older than 7 days and recreates the test_restore database. Use it only on a practice database.
